<article class="env-lemma" id="04VT">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04VT">Lemma <span data-tag="04VT">10.107.6</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R \to S$ is an epimorphism and finite, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$R \to S$ is surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      (This lemma seems to have been reproved many times in the literature, and has many different proofs.) It is clear that a surjective ring map is an epimorphism. Suppose that $R \to S$ is a finite ring map such that $S \otimes _ R S \to S$ is an isomorphism. Our goal is to show that $R \to S$ is surjective. Assume $S/R$ is not zero. The exact sequence $R \to S \to S/R \to 0$ leads to an exact sequence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R \otimes _ R S \to S \otimes _ R S \to S/R \otimes _ R S \to 0.  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Our assumption implies that the first arrow is an isomorphism, hence we conclude that $S/R \otimes _ R S = 0$. Hence also $S/R \otimes _ R S/R = 0$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00KZ" data-tag="00KZ">10.5.4</a> there exists a surjection of $R$-modules $S/R \to R/I$ for some proper ideal $I \subset R$. Hence there exists a surjection $S/R \otimes _ R S/R \to R/I \otimes _ R R/I = R/I \not= 0$, contradiction. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>